        <body>
            <div xml:id="d192">

                <pb facs="0561" n="533" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v03/pg533"/>

                <head>192. Letter From the Vice President of the Deutsche Bundesbank (Emminger) to the Under Secretary of
                    the Treasury for Monetary Affairs (Volcker)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d192-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Frankfurt</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1971-11-12">November 12, 1971</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Dear Paul:</p>
                <p>Attached please find my understanding of the present situation as concerns
                    exchange rate re-alignment. You will understand that I could not put in any more
                    concrete figures; but the attainable magnitudes have now become fairly
                    clear.</p>
                <p>This is, of course, only for your personal information.</p>
                <p>With best regards,</p>
                <p>sincerely</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Otmar Emminger</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d192-a1.xml" xpointer="d192-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d192-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d192-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Washington National Records Center, Department of the Treasury, Files of
                    Under Secretary Volcker: FRC 56 79 15, Germany
                    General. Personal. An attached note, dated November 16, reads: “Informed by Mr.
                        Bennett that he has been instructed
                    by Mr. Volcker to give Dr. Emminger an oral answer in Paris this week.” No
                    record of Volcker’s answer was found.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
